    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so if i'm looking at the beam sizing specifically, what are some of the common reasons or \"failure points\" where a structure might actually fail or give way under those loads? like what stuff should i be watching out for during the install to make sure things don't get compromised?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Common failure points in deck structures often involve unforeseen points of matter like insufficient fastening, which can compromise the overall integrity of the beam and ledger. Improperly anchored ledgers into floor joists or framing into concrete footings are also frequent issues that lead to structural collapse, especially during seismic events. During installation, it is critical to ensure ledger plates are tightly secured and that all post anchors are flush and corrosion-resistant, as these joints are the most susceptible to movement or failure under load. Ignoring these specifics during installation can render even the strongest western red cedar framing vulnerable to catastrophic failure when subjected to live loads.",
